"""Apply evidence-preserving sentence-review edits to the canonical TeX."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
OUT=ROOT/'review-revision/sentence-review-implementation'
actions=[]
main=(ROOT/'manuscript/main.tex').read_text()
app=(ROOT/'manuscript/appendix.tex').read_text()

def change(which,old,new,ids):
    global main,app
    s=main if which=='main' else app
    assert s.count(old)==1,(which,ids,old[:100],s.count(old))
    s=s.replace(old,new)
    if which=='main':main=s
    else:app=s
    actions.append({'file':'manuscript/'+('main.tex' if which=='main' else 'appendix.tex'),'review_ids':ids,'old':old,'new':new})

old=main.split('\\begin{abstract}\n')[1].split('\n\\end{abstract}')[0]
change('main',old,"""Replacing a reward model or translation metric can change which language outputs are selected. We ask how much candidate-evaluator score information is needed to certify a large retained subset under a budget on normalized average membership churn. The replacement family is finite, uniformly weighted and changes one evaluator at a time. A fixed cutoff makes each output's churn contribution computable from its own scores. Partial observations give a safely retainable count and an upper bound on the full-information optimum; their gap bounds retained-count regret. For odd-sized median pools, only selected outputs one vote from exclusion need queries. We characterize minimum offline entry certificate sizes at exact and positive-gap stopping by combining agreements that establish feasibility with oppositions proving that larger retained sets are infeasible. Whole-evaluator queries reveal scores for the entire batch, making certificate cost depend on evidence shared across outputs. Replays on RewardBench~2, WMT23 and three WMT24 language directions examine acquisition order, reference normalization and retention tolerance; controlled panels isolate evidence sharing. Zero-query retention includes invariant outputs and noninvariant outputs whose worst-case churn fits the budget. Tested mean-pooling strategies often inspect most active scores. Preference and MQM labels provide separate quality diagnostics; score-entry counts do not measure model execution cost.""",list(range(2,13)))
old=main.split('\\section{Introduction}\n')[1].split('\\section{Related Work}')[0]
change('main',old,r"""Reward models and translation metrics filter language outputs, but evaluator changes can alter the selected set \citep{malik2026rewardbench2,freitag2023wmt23,zheng2023judging}. Ensemble agreement can improve consistency without establishing output quality \citep{black2022selective,eisenstein2024herding}. A stability guarantee must specify the evaluator changes and membership decisions it covers.

A cutoff fitted on reference data selects outputs by base-pool consensus. Keeping it fixed, we consider replacing one pool member with a candidate from a declared finite set. A commitment mask identifies the outputs whose baseline inclusion or exclusion decisions we retain. We assess how often replacements disagree with these decisions; outputs outside the mask are referred for review. We maximize returned baseline-selected outputs under a budget on replacement-average churn, normalized by twice a fixed nominal selection count.

Complete candidate scores determine the largest feasible retained count. Partial scores leave this count uncertain. We ask how many observations are needed to certify it to a declared tolerance. Worst- and best-case completions give safely retainable and optimistic counts. Their gap bounds the possible loss in retained outputs; minimum certificate size measures the evidence needed to bound that gap.

This question connects to optimization with costly queries \citep{charikar2002priced,deshpande2016sbfe,goerigk2015robustqueries}. For odd-sized median pools, only selected outputs at the majority-vote boundary can lose membership. Their costs are proportional to sums of opposition bits. Minimum entry certificates combine evidence for feasibility with evidence ruling out larger retained sets. Positive tolerance changes the balance between these two requirements. Queries revealing one evaluator's scores for the entire batch additionally couple evidence across outputs. Offline certificates use hindsight; online strategies must discover the evidence.

NLP replays examine how budgets, evaluator composition and reference normalization change query requirements. Controlled panels hold row costs fixed while varying shared evidence. Preference and MQM labels assess retained-output quality separately.

""",list(range(13,23)))
