change('main','For each $i\\in C$, we commit'+old,r'The returned list is $S_0(P)\cap C$; decisions outside $C$ are referred for review. The budget bounds average membership changes by $2k\alpha$, not changes under each replacement separately. The fixed denominator makes $R(C)$ different from an error fraction among returned outputs; it can exceed one. Appendix~\ref{app:risk-interpretation} gives equivalent fixed normalizations. We maximize returned count subject to $R(C)\le\alpha$. Nonselected decisions add no value to this objective, so we commit them only when their cost is structurally zero.',[33,34])
change('main',r'If all candidate scores are available, Equation~\ref{eq:risk} is observable. Set $b_H=\lfloor2k\alpha H\rfloor$. Sorting selected outputs by $F_i$ and taking the largest prefix with cost at most $b_H$ solves the unit-profit budget problem. We denote this full-information optimum by $V^*$.',r'With all candidate scores observed, the costs $F_i$ are known. Set $b_H=\lfloor2k\alpha H\rfloor$. Each selected output has unit value, so sorting their costs and taking the longest prefix with total cost at most $b_H$ gives the full-information optimum $V^*$.',[35])
change('main','Within a frozen panel, base-pool and candidate-reference scores are visible. A query reveals one candidate--output test score; the visible base scores determine its $M$ replacement flips. Acquisition and stopping access only queried entries. After each path is fixed, a separate checker reads the complete matrix for retrospective evaluation.','Base-pool scores and candidate scores on the reference split are initially visible. Each query reveals one candidate--output test score, determining its $M$ replacement flips. Acquisition and stopping use this initial information and previously queried candidate test scores. After the path is fixed, a separate checker uses the complete matrix for retrospective evaluation.',[36])
